  "description": "Description of the Existing Building:\n\n- The current building is a Grade II listed, five window wide, Georgian house which is an end house in a terrace of three. It is situated in Leytonstone, London.\n- The current house contains a large, single family dwelling as well as two ancillary self-contained flats. One is situated at the basement level and one in a\nsmall existing ancillary building at the rear of the property. There is also a garden to the rear and a small rockery garden to the front of the property. \n- Situated within the grounds of the building is a former billiards or table sports hall out-building which was constructed in the Victorian period. This has been upgraded to contain three self contained flats and a garage. For the purposes of this planning application this building is not considered.\n\nDescription of the Proposed Conversion:\n\n- It is proposed that the building be converted from two flats and a large family dwelling to six self contained flats\n- Both other buildings in this terrace, numbers 696A and 698A Leytonstone High Road have received planning permission to be converted in a similar manner to\nthis proposal.\n- There is no net loss of family dwellings in the proposal\n- Minimal change to the external elevations or external appearance of the building is proposed excepting some work to create a new external landscaped courtyard at the\nfront of the building which will bring more light to the basement flat and improve access. This proposal will also bring a more rational and tidy appearance to\nthis area of the site.\n- The Historic character and fabric of the building are fully retained and enhanced and preserved for the future: The existing grand, central staircase is retained as part of proposal as well as large living spaces making use of generous floor to ceiling heights and existing sash windows (which are all also retained)\n- Existing doors, windows and stairs are retained as part of this proposal. Where existing doors are not required for the layout of the dwellings they are retained and sealed up. This will help to retain the historic character of the house both in the look and feel of the architecture in the shared hall / stairwell and the new dwellings themselves\n- All historic and existing Georgian features are retained including all cornices, room panelling stucco band above first floor, Georgian sash windows with plate\nglass and external Doric porches to both front and rear entrances, resulting in a conversion sympathetic to the appearance, historic character, history and\narchitecture of this building",
  "valid_date": "04/03/2024",
